  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nDangerous heat will impact the region over the weekend and \npotentially extending into early next week. The peak strength of the \nupper level ridge is forecast to occur on Friday and Saturday before \nweakening Sunday into Monday. Even though guidance is forecasting \nrecord H5 heights on Friday and Saturday, forecast highs are barely \nreaching daily records. This is either due to the modest low level \nmoisture remaining in place holding down daytime temperatures a bit, \nor the NBM is under forecasting temperatures. We are leaning toward \nthe NBM deterministic forecast temperatures not being hot enough, \nbut not enough to warrant nudging the forecast upward. It would not \nbe surprising to have actual highs closer to the 75-90th percentile \nof guidance, or a couple degrees higher than the current forecast. \nWe are likely to have some areas reaching 120 degrees across the \nlower deserts of southeast California on Saturday and Sunday, while \nthe Phoenix area could conceivably reach 118 or even 119 degrees. \nOvernight lows will also provide little relief from the extreme heat \nwith readings mostly in the upper 80s to the lower 90s to as high as \nthe mid 90s in the urban core of the Phoenix metro.\n\nMonsoon activity is expected to remain very limited on Saturday with \nlittle to no shot at any outflows nearing the lower deserts, but \nthat may change by Sunday or Monday. As the ridge begins to weaken \nSunday into Monday, guidance shows evidence of increasing low level \nmoisture and more monsoon activity across eastern Arizona and \nnorthern Mexico. However, chances still remain fairly low on Sunday \nand Monday with high terrain PoPs of 20-30% and 10-15% into the \nPhoenix area during the evening hours. As long as the ridge \ncontinues to weaken early next week, monsoon convection should also \nincrease, especially by the middle of next week as there are hints \nof a potential easterly wave moving into or near Arizona. As the \nridge weakens and moisture increases, temperatures are likely to \ndrift lower, but another day or two of Major to Extreme HeatRisk \nlooks possible for Monday and/or Tuesday, especially across \nsoutheast California and southwest Arizona.",
